ga9w(){return'The current time in the selected timezone as an analog clock with a 12-hour face. The hour hand turns twice a day; from noon on, "+12" appears below the 12.'},
ga9x(){return"The current time in the selected timezone as an analog clock with a 24-hour face. The hour hand turns once a day. Minutes and seconds are read on the outer scale, hours on the inner one."},
ga9y(){return"Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each decimal digit in binary (BCD, binary-coded decimal) in its own column of circles. Lit circles stand for 1, unlit circles for 0. The bottom circle is worth 2^0 = 1, above it 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc.; a digit is the sum of its lit circles. Tens digits need 2 or 3 bits, units digits 4."},
ga9z(){return"Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each as one binary number in its own column (5 bits for hours, 6 for minutes and seconds). Lit circles stand for 1, unlit circles for 0. The bottom circle is worth 2^0 = 1, above it 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc.; a number is the sum of its lit circles."},
ga9A(){return"Hours, minutes and seconds of the selected timezone as on a wall clock (24 hours of 60 minutes of 60 seconds), each written as a binary number with 5, 6 and 6 digits. The rightmost digit of a number is worth 2^0 = 1, to its left 2^1 = 2, 2^2 = 4 etc. Example: 10110:110000:010111 is 22:48:23."},
ga9B(){return"CCSDS Julian Date (CCSDS JD) = JD \u2212 2,436,204.5 (epoch: 1958-01-01 00:00:00 UT). Introduced by the Consultative Committee for Space Data Systems (CCSDS) for standardized spacecraft time codes, using 1958 as the historical reference date of International Atomic Time (TAI)."},
ga9C(){return"CNES Julian Date (CNES JD) = JD \u2212 2,433,282.5 (epoch: 1950-01-01 00:00:00 UT). Introduced by the French space agency CNES as a compact day count for spaceflight calculations, using the beginning of 1950 as its reference epoch."},
ga9D(){return"The current calendar date in the selected timezone, by default with the ISO week number (weeks start on Monday, week 1 contains the first Thursday) and the day of the year below."},
ga9E(){return"The current date and time in the selected timezone, combined in one value. Useful e.g. for world clock tabs and copying a complete timestamp to the clipboard."},
ga9F(){return"Minutes elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, from 0 to 1,439. On a day with a clock change the last minute is 1,379 or 1,499 (Lord Howe: 1,409 or 1,469; Troll: 1,319 or 1,559)."},
ga9G(){return"Percentage of the day elapsed in the selected timezone, from 0 to just under 100. It relates to the real length of the day: on a day with a clock change that is 23 or 25 hours (Lord Howe: 23.5 or 24.5; Troll: 22 or 26), not 24."},
ga9H(){return"Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, from 0 to 86,399. On a day with a clock change the last second is 82,799 or 89,999 (Lord Howe: 84,599 or 88,199; Troll: 79,199 or 93,599)."},
ga9I(){return"Decimal time, briefly in use in France in the 1790s, divides the day into 10 decimal hours of 100 decimal minutes of 100 decimal seconds, from 0h 00m 00s to 9h 99m 99s. On a normal day a decimal second lasts 0.864 s; on a day with a clock change the decimal day stretches or shrinks with the real day."},
ga9J(){return"The Doomsday Clock is a symbol of how close humanity is to self-destruction, set by the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists since 1947. Epoch shows the setting as a time of day; latest: 23:58:35, i.e. 85 seconds to midnight (January 2026). With Time Shift, historical settings are shown."},
ga9K(){return"Dublin Julian Date (DJD) = JD \u2212 2,415,020 (epoch: 1899-12-31 12:00:00 UT). Defined by the International Astronomical Union (IAU) in 1955 at its Dublin meeting as a convenient day count for astronomical calculations."},
ga9L(){return"Greenwich Mean Sidereal Time (GMST) \u2013 the hour angle of the vernal equinox at Greenwich, i.e. which part of the sky is overhead there. A sidereal day lasts 23 h 56 min 4.09 s, almost 4 minutes less than a solar day, so GMST gains about 3 min 56 s per day on the clock and runs through one more day than the clock over a year."},
ga9M(){return"GPS time counts seconds since 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC without any leap seconds. It matched UTC at the start; UTC has since added 18 leap seconds, so GPS time is 18 s ahead of UTC and 19 s behind TAI. From 2027 UTC adds no more leap seconds, so the gap stays at 18 s."},
ga9N(){return"Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, shown in base 16 (hexadecimal) and grouped in pairs from the right: from 0_00_00 to 1_51_7F on a normal day. A day with a clock change ends earlier or later."},
ga9O(){return"Julian Date (JD) counts days since noon on January 1, 4713 BC in the proleptic Julian calendar (epoch: -4712-01-01 12:00:00 UT). The standard time reference in astronomy for calculating intervals between events. A JD is the Julian day number plus the fraction of a day since the preceding noon UT; at midnight UT it therefore ends in .5."},
ga9P(){return"Local Mean Sidereal Time (LMST) \u2013 the hour angle of the vernal equinox at your location: GMST plus your longitude (15\xb0 = 1 hour)."},
ga9Q(){return"Modified Julian Date (MJD) = JD \u2212 2,400,000.5 (epoch: 1858-11-17 00:00:00 UT). Starts at midnight rather than noon, and has smaller numbers. Introduced by the Smithsonian Astrophysical Observatory in 1957 for satellite tracking, including the orbit of Sputnik."},
ga9R(){return"Modified Julian Date 2000 (MJD2000) = JD \u2212 2,451,544.5 or MJD \u2212 51,544 (epoch: 2000-01-01 00:00:00 UTC). Used in geodesy, geophysics, and spaceflight to simplify calculations with smaller numbers by shifting the reference to the beginning of the year 2000."},
ga9S(){return"New Earth Time (NET, 1999), also called degree time, splits the day into 360 degrees: 0\xb0 is midnight, 180\xb0 noon. A degree lasts 4 minutes and is divided into 60 intervals of 4 seconds. There are no time zones: the whole world shares the same degree time. It is based on UTC+0."},
ga9T(){return"Seconds elapsed since midnight in the selected timezone, shown in base 8 (octal) with six digits: from 000000 to 250577 on a normal day. A day with a clock change ends earlier or later."},
ga9U(){return"Reduced Julian Date (RJD) = JD \u2212 2,400,000 (epoch: 1858-11-16 12:00:00 UT). A simple reduction of the Julian Date used mainly in astronomy to make the numbers shorter, while retaining the original noon-based start of the Julian day."},
ga9V(){return"The current time in the selected timezone, shown in Roman numerals. Roman numerals have no zero, so, as medieval scholars did, N for nulla (Latin for 'none') stands in for it."},
ga9W(){return"The current time in the selected timezone in alarm clock design."},
ga9X(){return"Swatch Internet Time (1998) divides the day into 1000 .beats of 86.4 seconds each. There are no time zones: the whole world shares the same .beat time. It is based on UTC+1, which Swatch calls Biel Mean Time (BMT)."},
ga9Y(){return"International Atomic Time (TAI, French: Temps Atomique International) is a continuous scale without leap seconds: atomic clocks just keep ticking, and it is UTC that gets adjusted, so TAI is 37 s ahead of UTC. Epoch shows TAI the way Unix time counts (seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC), i.e. Unix time plus 37. According to a decision by the General Conference on Weights and Measures in October 2026, there will be no more leap seconds, so the 37 s stay."},
ga9Z(){return"The current time in the selected timezone, by default with the time zone abbreviation and UTC offset below. Within 7 days before a clock change, a notice about it replaces them."},
gaa_(){return'Seconds since the Unix epoch, 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC. Unix time counts every day as exactly 86,400 seconds and ignores leap seconds. A signed 32-bit counter overflows on 2038-01-19 at 03:14:07 UTC (the "Year 2038 problem"); Time Shift stops there, too.'},
